-- emit: §8.5's emission order — TWO sorted runs, every `$$order` table
-- first and then every function (`docs/design/static-dispatch-spike.md`
-- §8.5, §9.6, A.29).
--
-- Ordering within the function run never matters: every derived function
-- is an arrow, so a reference from one to another is resolved when it is
-- CALLED and a forward reference is fine.
--
-- The `$$order` tables are the one exception §8.5 names, because they are
-- object literals rather than arrows. One sort cannot give it:
-- `Amber$$compare` sorts BEFORE `Amber$$order` by name text — `c` before
-- `o` — which is precisely the wrong way round, and here both tables come
-- first. Hence two runs, each sorted by printed name text.
--
-- What the interleaving shows. By name text the eleven synthesised values
-- would be, in one run:
--
--     Amber$$compare  Amber$$eq  Amber$$order  Zinc$$compare  Zinc$$eq
--     Zinc$$order  compare$prim  compare$r$hue$name  eq$prim
--     eq$r$hue$name
--
-- and the file below is that list with the two `$$order` rows lifted out
-- in front, in their own sorted run. Nominal bases sort ahead of
-- structural ones for free: a module path segment is upper-case and every
-- structural base is lower-case (A.61).
--
-- Sorted by NAME and not by the order the rows were discovered in, which
-- is deterministic today but not obviously so, and CLAUDE.md rule 5 asks
-- for an order a reader can check.
import Node exposing (Program)
import String


pub type Amber
    = Dawn
    | Dusk


pub type Zinc
    = Plate Int
    | Ingot Int Int


pub sorted : { hue : Int, name : String }, { hue : Int, name : String } -> Bool
sorted a b = a < b


pub same : { hue : Int, name : String }, { hue : Int, name : String } -> Bool
same a b = a == b


main : Program
main = Node.printLines []
